#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""zstd-overlay: transparent zstd compression for folders on a Linux VM, built
for Claude Code cloud sessions (30 GB disk, root, kernel with btrfs and
overlayfs). Python 3.11+, standard library only; installs nothing: an empty
btrfs image ships at the end of this file, and the kernel does the rest.

Every write under a mounted folder lands in a compressed btrfs image. What the
folder held before stays where it was and stays readable. Programs see plain
files; pnpm, cargo, uv, dotnet and the rest need no flags.

Usage:
  zstd-overlay up [DIR...]         mount DIRs (default list when none is given)
  zstd-overlay up --merge DIR...   mount DIR although it changed while unmounted;
                                   the changed files win (look first: `doctor`)
  zstd-overlay up --hook [DIR...]  as up, for a SessionStart hook: exits 0 and
                                   reports refused folders in JSON
  zstd-overlay migrate DIR...      move what DIR already holds into the image
  zstd-overlay install             copy to /usr/local/bin and add a SessionStart hook
  zstd-overlay status              disk use, capacity, mounted folders
  zstd-overlay doctor              what is mounted, what is not, and why
  zstd-overlay version             this file's SHA-256 (12 hex digits), commit and date

Default list: the folder holding $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR (in a cloud session
/home/user, so every repository, also one attached later, is covered; the
repository itself if that folder is a protected one), ~/.cache, ~/.local/share,
~/.local/bin, ~/.npm, and whichever of ~/.rustup ~/.cargo ~/go ~/.m2 ~/.gradle
~/.nuget ~/.dotnet ~/.bun exist.

A VM restore drops every mount, so `install` runs `up --hook` from a
SessionStart hook. No write made while a folder was not mounted is hidden under
the image: where the image has its own version of such a file, the folder that
holds the repositories is not mounted (`up` names the files and leaves it on
the normal disk until you decide). A cache folder stays empty on the normal
disk: what is written there while unmounted moves into the image before the
mount. Each repository's .git stays on the normal disk, bind-mounted into the
folder.

The image reserves its disk space up front (fallocate), leaving
ZSTD_OVERLAY_RESERVE_MB free outside it. A disk that fills from outside then
gives the writer outside a plain "No space left"; the image, whose blocks are
already its own, keeps working. (A sparse image would take that error itself,
as an I/O error, and btrfs would turn read-only.)

Environment: ZSTD_OVERLAY_IMG (default /var/lib/zstd-overlay/disk.img),
ZSTD_OVERLAY_MNT (default /mnt/zstd-overlay), ZSTD_OVERLAY_RESERVE_MB (4096).

Exit status: 0 done; 2 an error stopped it; 3 some folders were refused.
"""

